---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="MAME Cheats | ArcadeDuck"
  description="Set up and use native MAME XML cheats in ArcadeDuck with cheat.7z or loose shortname XML files."
>
  <section class="page-hero cheat-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
    <h1>Native MAME cheats</h1>
    <p class="page-description">
      ArcadeDuck supports the MAME XML cheat format natively. Use the standard
      MAME cheat package or loose XML files named for each game's MAME shortname.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section cheat-docs">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Setup</p>
        <h2>Put compatible cheat files in ArcadeDuck's cheats folder</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        ArcadeDuck provides MAME cheat-format compatibility, but cheat databases
        are not bundled. Supply your own compatible files.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="planned-system-grid">
      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Package</span>
        <h3>Use <code>cheat.7z</code></h3>
        <pre><code>ArcadeDuck/
    cheats/
        cheat.7z</code></pre>
        <p>
          ArcadeDuck looks inside the standard MAME cheat archive for an XML
          member matching the current arcade set's MAME shortname.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Loose XML</span>
        <h3>Use <code>&lt;shortname&gt;.xml</code></h3>
        <pre><code>ArcadeDuck/
    cheats/
        cryptklr.xml
        tekken.xml</code></pre>
        <p>
          Loose XML filenames use the game's MAME shortname. An exact loose XML
          file takes precedence over the matching entry in <code>cheat.7z</code>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section cheat-docs">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Lookup</p>
        <h2>Exact set first, then parent fallback</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      ArcadeDuck first checks for <code>cheats\&lt;shortname&gt;.xml</code>, then
      for <code>&lt;shortname&gt;.xml</code> inside <code>cheats\cheat.7z</code>.
      If neither exact definition exists and the game is a clone, ArcadeDuck
      repeats that lookup using the parent set's MAME shortname.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section cheat-docs">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Using Cheats</p>
        <h2>Qt Cheat Manager and fullscreen Cheat List</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        The same native MAME cheats are available from both ArcadeDuck user
        interfaces while a supported arcade game is running.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="planned-system-grid">
      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Desktop UI</span>
        <h3>Cheats &gt; Cheat Manager</h3>
        <p>
          Open the normal Qt Cheat Manager to toggle on/off cheats, activate
          one-shot entries, and move through numeric or item-list parameter values.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Fullscreen UI</span>
        <h3>Pause menu &gt; Cheat List</h3>
        <p>
          The fullscreen cheat list provides the same controls for cabinet use.
          Text-only MAME entries appear as headings or separators instead of fake
          selectable cheats.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p>
      When a MAME cheat uses <code>&lt;output&gt;</code>, ArcadeDuck renders that
      text over the game. This supports dynamic output generated by the cheat,
      including argument substitution and line positioning/alignment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section cheat-docs">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Format Support</p>
        <h2>MAME XML functionality</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        ArcadeDuck implements the MAME XML version 1 functionality used by the
        current MAME cheat package for PS1-derived arcade hardware.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-list">
      <span>On/off cheats</span>
      <span>One-shot cheats</span>
      <span>Numeric parameters</span>
      <span>Item/list parameters</span>
      <span><code>on</code>, <code>off</code>, <code>run</code>, and <code>change</code> scripts</span>
      <span>MAME-style expressions and temporary variables</span>
      <span><code>param</code>, <code>frame</code>, and <code>argindex</code></span>
      <span>Conditional actions</span>
      <span><code>&lt;output&gt;</code> entries and arguments</span>
      <span>MAME <code>maincpu</code> logical memory access</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section cheat-docs">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Troubleshooting</p>
        <h2>If cheats do not appear</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="troubleshooting-grid">
      <article>
        <h3>Check the cheat setting</h3>
        <p>
          ArcadeDuck's normal cheat-enable setting controls whether MAME cheats
          are loaded.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Check the shortname</h3>
        <p>
          Loose XML filenames and archive members must use the MAME shortname,
          not the game's display title.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Check the log</h3>
        <p>
          Invalid individual cheat entries are disabled and logged rather than
          taking down the entire loaded cheat list.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-note cheat-legacy-note">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Legacy Formats</p>
      <h2>ArcadeDuck uses MAME cheats now.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      The old GameShark/PCSXR/ePSXe/libretro-style <code>.cht</code> system,
      manual GameShark code entry, cheat importing/exporting, the built-in
      <code>chtdb.txt</code> database, and the old Cheat Code Editor have been
      removed. Memory Scanner and Memory Watch remain available as separate
      memory-analysis features.
    </p>
  </section>

  <style>
    .cheat-hero { max-width: 940px; }

    .cheat-docs > p {
      max-width: 900px;
      color: var(--text-muted);
      line-height: 1.7;
    }

    .cheat-docs pre {
      overflow-x: auto;
      margin: 18px 0;
      padding: 18px 20px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 10px;
      background: rgba(18, 16, 28, 0.78);
    }

    .cheat-docs pre code,
    .cheat-docs p code,
    .cheat-legacy-note code,
    .planned-system-card code,
    .feature-list code {
      color: #d9c8ff;
      font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Consolas, monospace;
    }

    .feature-list {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 10px;
    }

    .feature-list > span,
    .troubleshooting-grid > article {
      padding: 16px 18px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 10px;
      background: rgba(18, 16, 28, 0.55);
      color: var(--text-muted);
      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    .troubleshooting-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 14px;
    }

    .troubleshooting-grid h3 {
      margin: 0 0 8px;
      color: var(--text-main);
      font-size: 1rem;
    }

    .troubleshooting-grid p {
      margin: 0;
    }
  </style>
</SiteLayout>
